===Etymology===
Circa 1959, [[MIT]] {{w|Tech Model Railroad Club}}.<ref>{{w|Peter Samson}}, [http://www.gricer.com/tmrc/dictionary1959.html AN ABRIDGED DICTIONARY of the TMRC LANGUAGE] June 1959 (with 2005 commentary)</ref> {{unk|en}} origin; possibly from Cruft Hall, built in 1915 as a gift from a donor named Harriet Otis Cruft.<ref>{{cite-web|url=http://www.thecrimson.com/article/1916/3/10/bronze-tablet-erected-in-cruft-memorial/|title=Bronze Tablet Erected in Cruft Memorial Laboratory|site=The Harvard Crimson|accessdate=26 November 2014}}</ref> Cruft Hall was the radar laboratory of {{w|Harvard}}'s physics department during the Second World War, which contained much old and unused technical equipment. Possibly {{blend|en|crust|fluff|nocap=1}}, both of which may form on old abandoned things, or influenced by {{m|en|crud}}.

